        <body>
            <div xml:id="d633">

                <pb facs="1098" n="998" xml:id="pg998"/>

                <head>No. 633.<lb/><hi rendition="#italic">Mr. Farman to Mr. Payson.</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Consulate-General of the United States in Egypt</hi>,<lb/><hi
                                rendition="#italic">Cairo</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1879-11-28"><hi rendition="#italic"
                                >November</hi> 28, 1879</date>. (Received December 17.)</dateline>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 352.]</seg></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: You will remember, when I had the pleasure
                    of seeing you last, that I mentioned that the United States bad no prison in
                    Egypt, and desired to know in what prison any person that should be sentenced by
                    the United States consular court in Egypt to imprisonment for a term of years,
                    could be confined.</p>
                <p>Will you please give me instructions in this respect? I should like also to be
                    informed as to whether or not the prison for such purpose has facilities for
                    providing the convicts with labor, so that they can be sentenced to “hard labor”
                    as well as confinement.</p>
                <p>I would thank you to send me an answer to this by the first mail.</p>
                <p>I have, &amp;c.,</p>
                <closer><signed>E. E. FARMAN.</signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
